WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:08.088
The construction industry

00:00:08.088 --> 00:00:11.967
accounts for 30 to 40 percent

00:00:12.008 --> 00:00:16.137
of the world's total energy and resources.

00:00:16.137 --> 00:00:21.935
It also accounts for 40 percent
of the world's total carbon emissions.

00:00:23.144 --> 00:00:28.358
A man who lived long before us
gave us a very simple solution.

00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:33.947
And he is Mahatma Gandhi,
the father of our nation, India.

00:00:33.947 --> 00:00:35.115
He said

00:00:35.156 --> 00:00:38.201
that the ideal house should be built

00:00:38.243 --> 00:00:42.914
with materials found in a five-mile
radius around the house.

00:00:42.914 --> 00:00:46.001
So you think, OK, what is that material?

00:00:46.459 --> 00:00:49.421
And it is right below our feet.

00:00:49.462 --> 00:00:50.714
It's mud.

00:00:50.714 --> 00:00:55.093
Now imagine, if we can build
a house with mud,

00:00:55.093 --> 00:00:59.306
there would be nothing more sustainable
or eco-friendly than that.

00:01:00.015 --> 00:01:03.226
But then, you might ask,

00:01:03.268 --> 00:01:04.686
how strong is mud?

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.608
This compressed five percent
cement-added mud block,

00:01:09.649 --> 00:01:14.321
made by my alma mater,
Auroville Earth Institute,

00:01:14.362 --> 00:01:17.699
is twice as strong
as a country fired brick.

00:01:17.741 --> 00:01:24.706
In fact, it has a dry compressive
strength of 6.5 megapascal,

00:01:24.706 --> 00:01:30.295
as compared to a normal fired-brick,
which just has 3 to 3.5 megapascal.

00:01:31.379 --> 00:01:33.840
But then you may again ask,

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.258
what about water?

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.011
Wouldn't water destroy mud brick?

00:01:38.053 --> 00:01:41.431
This is a brick from the same institute

00:01:41.473 --> 00:01:46.144
kept in water since 1995,

00:01:46.186 --> 00:01:48.605
a little less than 30 years,

00:01:48.605 --> 00:01:49.981
and you can see

00:01:50.023 --> 00:01:53.818
that it can still hold
a significant amount of load.

00:01:54.653 --> 00:01:56.404
So what does this tell us?

00:01:56.946 --> 00:01:59.991
Can we change our perception
of construction?

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:04.329
Can we imagine a new way of building?

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:08.333
Or can we bring beauty to dirt?

00:02:11.127 --> 00:02:16.466
I demoted myself from being
an architecture graduate

00:02:16.508 --> 00:02:18.301
to being that of a mason.

00:02:18.885 --> 00:02:21.137
Or maybe I promoted myself, I don't know.

00:02:22.138 --> 00:02:24.808
I joined hands with local workers

00:02:24.849 --> 00:02:28.019
and started teaching them mud techniques.

00:02:28.395 --> 00:02:34.025
Very soon we started to change
every aspect of our construction.

00:02:35.026 --> 00:02:40.740
Our foundations became poured earth
or rammed earth,

00:02:40.782 --> 00:02:46.454
our walls became mud blocks
or compressed earth bricks.

00:02:46.496 --> 00:02:53.128
Why, even our roofs became domes
and vaults made of mud bricks.

00:02:53.586 --> 00:02:59.801
This is a brick wall residence
called Pirouette House,

00:02:59.801 --> 00:03:03.096
which we made in a very crowded community.

00:03:03.138 --> 00:03:08.226
So it goes in a zigzag pattern
in need of natural ventilation.

00:03:09.227 --> 00:03:15.692
Now, please remember
that this wonderful parametric form,

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:17.235
or curves,

00:03:17.277 --> 00:03:22.532
were made not with any
computational technology, AI

00:03:22.532 --> 00:03:25.201
or robotic arms.

00:03:26.035 --> 00:03:32.751
These were actually made
with coconut twigs, thread

00:03:32.792 --> 00:03:35.712
and the masons' bear arms.

00:03:35.754 --> 00:03:41.718
At a time when the building
industry is fast moving

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.472
towards a future that exceeds mankind,

00:03:45.472 --> 00:03:51.019
we brought the value
of human resources back into it.

00:03:51.478 --> 00:03:53.730
But way back in 2012,

00:03:53.772 --> 00:03:58.401
I was posed with a very
life-altering question.

00:03:58.401 --> 00:04:03.865
I was asked to build a residence,
a low-budget, eco-friendly residence

00:04:03.865 --> 00:04:07.076
for a primary school teacher
called Biju Mathew.

00:04:07.702 --> 00:04:11.331
And when I reached the site,
this was what I saw.

00:04:11.956 --> 00:04:15.794
A mountain of waste and debris

00:04:15.835 --> 00:04:20.548
dumped by the neighbors
after they finished their construction.

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:22.801
And because there was no other empty plot.

00:04:23.676 --> 00:04:24.886
So what do I do?

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:32.227
Do I stand back and allow
this waste to be dumped elsewhere?

00:04:32.769 --> 00:04:37.649
Or do I reimagine
the philosophy of building

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.235
with materials found in my vicinity?

00:04:41.611 --> 00:04:46.950
Neither me nor the future
generations can unsee the waste

00:04:46.991 --> 00:04:50.620
that is getting piled in our vicinity.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:56.125
Therefore, we decided to use this waste

00:04:56.167 --> 00:04:58.837
as a part of our construction technique.

00:04:58.878 --> 00:05:02.757
We hand-pulverized this material,

00:05:02.799 --> 00:05:06.052
added a bit of soil and again,

00:05:06.094 --> 00:05:07.929
five-percent cement.

00:05:07.929 --> 00:05:11.975
And using the techniques
we learned so far,

00:05:12.016 --> 00:05:14.811
we kind of poured it in between two meshes

00:05:14.811 --> 00:05:16.980
and poured the entire mixture into it.

00:05:17.021 --> 00:05:22.610
The resultant product
was not just a formidable partition.

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:26.155
It was load-bearing,
means these walls

00:05:26.281 --> 00:05:30.577
took the load
of the entire roof as well.

00:05:30.994 --> 00:05:35.081
But this came at a price.

00:05:35.748 --> 00:05:40.295
I was nicknamed “scrap engineer” by locals

00:05:40.295 --> 00:05:45.967
who found it amusing to see an engineer
search through scrap in the junkyard.

00:05:46.342 --> 00:05:52.056
But what I found
in the junkyard was pure gold:

00:05:52.098 --> 00:05:56.728
this is discarded electric meter boxes,

00:05:56.728 --> 00:06:00.106
washing machine wheels,

00:06:00.148 --> 00:06:04.569
decaying wood, all became part
of a new philosophy

00:06:04.611 --> 00:06:07.697
that included more and more waste.

00:06:09.073 --> 00:06:10.241
Thank you.

00:06:10.241 --> 00:06:13.411
(Applause)

00:06:13.453 --> 00:06:16.164
Now, emboldened by this,

00:06:16.164 --> 00:06:20.418
our new aim was to build
multi-storied mud and waste construction.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:22.170
Is that possible? Yes.

00:06:22.211 --> 00:06:25.715
This is Shikhara, a three-storeyed

00:06:25.757 --> 00:06:29.510
multi-story, mud and waste construction.

00:06:29.886 --> 00:06:35.308
We decided to further modify
what we did in the Biju Mathew residence.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:38.227
So instead of using two meshes,

00:06:38.227 --> 00:06:41.105
we put two wooden shutters

00:06:41.147 --> 00:06:45.443
and poured the same mixture
which we did in the earlier project.

00:06:45.485 --> 00:06:50.865
And the resultant product
was as strong as before

00:06:50.865 --> 00:06:52.825
and economical too.

00:06:53.201 --> 00:06:58.790
This wall had a dry compressive
strength of 4.5 megapascal.

00:06:59.332 --> 00:07:03.378
And mind you, this building,
this residence,

00:07:03.378 --> 00:07:07.715
we completed in a meager budget
of 90,000 dollars

00:07:07.715 --> 00:07:11.511
as compared to a general budget
of 200,000 dollars or more.

00:07:11.886 --> 00:07:13.012
Very soon --

00:07:13.054 --> 00:07:14.931
(Applause)

00:07:14.973 --> 00:07:18.810
Very soon, many practices in the country

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.354
started adopting this technique,

00:07:21.354 --> 00:07:25.274
not just because of its eco-friendly part,

00:07:25.274 --> 00:07:27.860
but also because it was cost-effective.

00:07:28.444 --> 00:07:30.279
For us Indians,

00:07:30.279 --> 00:07:32.740
born to traditional societies,

00:07:32.782 --> 00:07:39.539
very often, our dreams culminate
in lavish weddings and festivals.

00:07:40.039 --> 00:07:42.667
This, by the way, is a wedding stage.

00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:47.005
But the very next day,
this too, is a common scene.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:54.053
India discards 3.6 million tons of plastic

00:07:54.095 --> 00:07:55.221
every year.

00:07:55.930 --> 00:08:01.936
Therefore, we decided
to utilize plastic bottles

00:08:01.936 --> 00:08:06.566
as a part of our construction
for a new project called Chuzhi.

00:08:06.607 --> 00:08:07.984
It's a housing project.

00:08:08.359 --> 00:08:13.906
And in this project,
we started to build circular beams

00:08:13.948 --> 00:08:17.243
using plastic bottles and reinforced
concrete and of course, mud.

00:08:17.618 --> 00:08:19.912
We started to build it in circles.

00:08:20.747 --> 00:08:23.583
To be honest, we didn’t know
how this would eventually look,

00:08:23.624 --> 00:08:25.918
but we still kept on building the circles.

00:08:25.918 --> 00:08:27.253
(Laughter)

00:08:27.295 --> 00:08:30.715
We made it stable
and put a roof on top of it.

00:08:30.715 --> 00:08:33.176
You can see how weird this looks.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:40.099
Chuzhi is a project
that resides in a rocky terrain.

00:08:40.975 --> 00:08:44.812
And it's populated
by a lot of trees and shrubs.

00:08:45.396 --> 00:08:46.856
Now, generally,

00:08:46.856 --> 00:08:51.736
these two factors are attributed
as very bad things for construction.

00:08:52.487 --> 00:08:56.491
People generally cut trees

00:08:56.532 --> 00:09:02.163
or clear them to make spaces for their
rooms or buildings or whatever.

00:09:02.872 --> 00:09:04.332
But in here,

00:09:04.332 --> 00:09:07.502
the trees not only form shelter

00:09:07.502 --> 00:09:12.006
but also cool the building
in a tropical climate.

00:09:12.465 --> 00:09:14.509
And as far as the terrain was concerned,

00:09:14.509 --> 00:09:18.429
generally, people would fill it up,
level it, clear it

00:09:18.471 --> 00:09:19.639
and build on top of it.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.224
But here,

00:09:21.224 --> 00:09:25.311
the building is perfectly camouflaged

00:09:25.353 --> 00:09:26.896
in the rocky terrain,

00:09:26.896 --> 00:09:30.441
that 10 metres away from this project,
you will not see it.

00:09:30.483 --> 00:09:32.610
That is how good it is camouflaged.

00:09:33.277 --> 00:09:34.737
In this project --

00:09:34.737 --> 00:09:37.490
(Applause)

00:09:37.532 --> 00:09:42.495
We managed to use 4,300 plastic bottles

00:09:42.537 --> 00:09:45.581
which would have otherwise
ended up as a landfill

00:09:45.581 --> 00:09:47.458
or even worse, in the ocean.

00:09:48.668 --> 00:09:52.463
One day, we all got sick
working at the site.

00:09:52.505 --> 00:09:56.801
We had breathing difficulty
and we kind of went to the doctor.

00:09:57.093 --> 00:10:00.096
And the doctor very calmly said,

00:10:00.096 --> 00:10:03.850
"Ah, it must be the burning tires
used in road construction.

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:05.268
Stay away."

00:10:06.686 --> 00:10:08.104
And we were shocked.

00:10:09.438 --> 00:10:12.650
There are hundreds of kids
who travel these roads

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:14.902
every day to their schools.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:18.990
There are thousands of families
who traverse these roads

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:20.199
every day to their jobs,

00:10:20.199 --> 00:10:22.577
why some of us even stay on these roads.

00:10:23.953 --> 00:10:26.414
And the answer is stay away.

00:10:27.623 --> 00:10:30.334
Upon further investigation,

00:10:30.376 --> 00:10:32.837
adding to our worst nightmare,

00:10:32.837 --> 00:10:37.425
we found out that our country discards

00:10:37.425 --> 00:10:41.179
half a million tires in a day.

00:10:41.179 --> 00:10:42.638
(Audience murmurs)

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:44.265
No, no, I'm not done yet.

00:10:44.307 --> 00:10:50.605
And the rest of the world discards
another half a million tires

00:10:50.646 --> 00:10:52.356
to India on the same day.

00:10:52.356 --> 00:10:54.066
(Audience murmurs)

00:10:54.066 --> 00:10:55.234
Yeah.

00:10:55.276 --> 00:10:59.572
This is an aerial pic of a tire dump,

00:10:59.614 --> 00:11:01.032
seen from space.

00:11:01.449 --> 00:11:04.035
This is in a GCC country,

00:11:04.076 --> 00:11:07.371
that collects the tires
from all of you guys,

00:11:07.371 --> 00:11:10.458
and eventually, dumps it
in countries like India.

00:11:11.334 --> 00:11:13.461
Now you should remember,

00:11:13.461 --> 00:11:17.048
all of these tires, most of them at least,

00:11:17.089 --> 00:11:22.136
eventually end up either
in the road tarring illegal process

00:11:22.136 --> 00:11:26.182
or we end up burning it
to make again fuel oil,

00:11:26.182 --> 00:11:28.059
because this is a petroleum product.

00:11:29.268 --> 00:11:36.192
Now, one way to neutralize
this huge toxic threat

00:11:36.234 --> 00:11:38.736
is to use it as part of construction.

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:44.116
Now inspired from architects
like Michael Reynolds,

00:11:44.116 --> 00:11:48.412
we introduced mud and tire

00:11:48.412 --> 00:11:52.625
to form a new building
construction technique.

00:11:53.376 --> 00:11:57.588
And now we are building residences,

00:11:57.630 --> 00:12:02.301
we are building museums, schools.

00:12:02.301 --> 00:12:05.388
And this is the best way
to neutralize this threat

00:12:05.429 --> 00:12:07.598
and create beautiful construction.

00:12:08.307 --> 00:12:12.520
We believe that tomorrow this may also be

00:12:12.561 --> 00:12:17.358
the alternative for the housing crisis
that is a big reality

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:19.819
for many of our underdeveloped countries.

00:12:20.903 --> 00:12:24.448
The heart of every project of ours

00:12:24.448 --> 00:12:28.452
lies with the problems
that we created amongst us.

00:12:28.494 --> 00:12:30.997
Discarded tires,

00:12:30.997 --> 00:12:33.291
plastic bottles,

00:12:33.291 --> 00:12:35.042
construction debris.

00:12:35.584 --> 00:12:39.297
None of these things
should be dumped thoughtlessly

00:12:39.297 --> 00:12:40.506
or carelessly.

00:12:41.173 --> 00:12:46.220
The world can not just find a solution,

00:12:46.262 --> 00:12:49.890
but we can even find beauty

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:52.852
in this disaster
that we created for ourselves.

00:12:53.311 --> 00:12:56.105
I remember a single line

00:12:56.105 --> 00:12:59.608
by the poet of my native language, Ulloor,

00:12:59.650 --> 00:13:01.319
and it goes like this.

00:13:01.694 --> 00:13:06.365
(Speaking Malayalam)

00:13:06.407 --> 00:13:08.200
And it translates to this:

00:13:08.242 --> 00:13:09.910
It is our choice

00:13:09.952 --> 00:13:13.998
whether to build heaven or hell on Earth.

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:15.458
Thank you, thank you.

00:13:15.458 --> 00:13:22.089
(Applause and cheers)

